WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.473
The NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, often called CSF 2.0, helps organizations manage and reduce cybersecurity risk.

2
00:00:08.473 --> 00:00:19.770
It gives teams a shared language for discussing cybersecurity outcomes, so executives, managers, engineers, security teams, auditors, suppliers, and partners can talk about risk

3
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:24.477
without forcing every conversation into tool names or control numbers.

4
00:00:24.477 --> 00:00:33.891
That shared language is useful because cybersecurity decisions often involve tradeoffs between mission, technology, cost, time, resilience, and risk tolerance.

5
00:00:33.891 --> 00:00:37.186
CSF 2.0 is flexible by design.

6
00:00:37.186 --> 00:00:43.776
It is intended for organizations of different sizes, sectors, technology environments, and maturity levels.

7
00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:48.484
Its main components are the Core, Organizational Profiles, and Tiers.

8
00:00:48.484 --> 00:00:56.486
A startup, hospital, manufacturer, school district, cloud team, or government program may all use the framework differently.

9
00:00:56.486 --> 00:01:04.488
The framework does not say that every organization must implement the same controls in the same order.

10
00:01:04.488 --> 00:01:10.607
Instead, it describes high-level outcomes that an organization can tailor to its context.

11
00:01:10.607 --> 00:01:16.256
It is also important to understand what CSF 2.0 is not.

12
00:01:16.256 --> 00:01:22.375
It is not a certification by itself, and it does not guarantee security.

13
00:01:22.375 --> 00:01:30.377
It is not a rigid checklist that replaces engineering judgment, legal review, control design, or operational ownership.

14
00:01:30.377 --> 00:01:38.850
It helps organizations understand current risk, set target outcomes, prioritize improvement, communicate decisions, and track progress over time.

15
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.028
Used well, CSF 2.0 becomes a practical risk management tool.

16
00:01:44.028 --> 00:01:55.325
It helps an owner explain why an improvement plan matters, why one gap is more urgent than another, what evidence supports the current assessment,

17
00:01:55.325 --> 00:01:58.149
and how cybersecurity supports business objectives.

18
00:01:58.149 --> 00:02:01.444
The value is not in claiming alignment.

19
00:02:01.444 --> 00:02:07.093
The value is in clearer decisions, better prioritization, and honest continuous improvement.
